==English==
{{was wotd|2016|June|20}}
[[File:Collodi Pinocchio.JPG|thumb|A giant statue of [[Pinocchio]] in a park called Parco di Pinocchio, in Collodi, [[Pescia]], Italy. According to the story ''{{w|The Adventures of Pinocchio}}'' (1883) by Italian author {{w|Carlo Collodi}}, Pinocchio is a wooden puppet whose nose grows longer when he tells lies.]]

===Etymology===
Believed to be from a quotation by the British-Irish statesman and philosopher {{w|Edmund Burke}} (1729–1797): “Falsehood and delusion are allowed in no case whatever: but, as in the exercise of all the virtues, there is an economy of truth. It is a sort of temperance, by which a man speaks truth with measure that he may speak it the longer.”

===Pronunciation===
* {{hyphenation|eco|no|mi|cal with the truth|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{head|en|adjective}}

# {{label|en|idiomatic|euphemistic}} Not [[telling]] the [[whole]] truth, especially in order to present a [[false]] [[image]] of a situation; [[untruthful]]; [[lying]]. {{n-g|Often used with [[sarcasm]] or [[satire]].}}
#: {{ux|en|I would be being '''economical with the truth''' if I were to tell you that I was enjoying myself.}}

